Field teams rarely catalog on a single device. NAHPU supports three
[field workflows](/en/usages/workflows/), and these tutorials teach the two
that put several catalogers on several devices:

- **[Workflow A](/en/usages/workflows/#workflow-a): shared site, parallel
  catalogs.** Specimens come from a shared site and collecting event—a
  mist-net check, a pitfall line, a Malaise trap—and each person catalogs the
  specimens they sort on their own device.
- **[Workflow B](/en/usages/workflows/#workflow-b): separate sites, parallel
  catalogs.** Each person runs their own trap line, then prepares and catalogs
  its catch on their own device.

In both, everyone needs the same project and people, each person keeps a
separate specimen catalog, and the catalogs are merged into one project at the
end. Real teams often mix the two, and so do these tutorials.

A pair works the same Mt. Tujuh survey from the
[Basic tutorials](/en/tutorials/basic/paper-catalog/):

- **Heru** (on an iPad) creates the project and the shared site catalog.
- **Bima** (on an iPhone) copies them by scanning QR codes.
- **Workflow A:** both catalog the morning check of Line 1 on 13 September 2014
  in parallel.
- **Workflow B:** Bima also runs Line 2 on his own and catalogs its catch.
- Heru merges Bima's catalog, checks all tissues into one freezer box, and
  exports a Darwin Core Archive.

{% aside type="note" title="A re-enactment of real records" %}
In 2014, Heru cataloged every specimen himself. These tutorials imagine that
Bima Sampel (a fictional teammate) cataloged the pitfall catch and ran Line 2.
Bima's five records are real specimens from the paper catalog—HH 451 to HH 454
and HH 456—renumbered `BS1` to `BS5` for teaching. All measurements, dates, and tissue numbers are as
written on the paper forms.
{% /aside %}

Before starting, work through
[Basic tutorials 2–5](/en/tutorials/basic/project-personnel-taxa/), which
explain each form used here. Allow about an hour for the series. You need
two NAHPU installations, such as a tablet and a phone. Scanning QR codes works
on Android, iOS, macOS, and the web; on other devices, use the JSON export and
import shown in [Teamwork tutorial 3: Share events and taxa](/en/tutorials/teamwork/share-events-and-taxa/).

